+## Phase 3 — the signal/slot conversion (the bulk of the work)
+
+v0.7 contains **626 new-style `connect()` calls across 77 files, 21 new-style
+`disconnect()` calls, and 11 more connects in `tests/`** (v0.6 had zero — all of them
+appeared in this window). Qt4 cannot compile any of them. Convert every one to the
+string-based syntax, which works identically on Qt4 and Qt5 — **no `#if` guards, one
+code path**:
+
+```cpp
+// from:
+connect(m_model, &Imap::Mailbox::Model::connectionError, this, &MainWindow::slotShowError);
+// to:
+connect(m_model, SIGNAL(connectionError(QString)), this, SLOT(slotShowError(QString)));
+```
+
+Rules — these matter, because **old-style connect failures are silent at compile time
+and only warn at runtime**; every sloppy signature is an invisibly broken feature:
+
+1. **Look up every signature** in the class header before writing the macro. Never
+   guess parameter lists. The SIGNAL/SLOT string must contain the parameter *types*
+   only — no parameter names, no `const`/`&` (write `SIGNAL(changed(QString))`, not
+   `SIGNAL(changed(const QString &name))`; Qt normalizes, but write it normalized
+   yourself so grep-audits work).
+2. If the target member is not declared as a slot/signal in its header (new-style
+   connect never needed that), **add it to the appropriate `slots:` section** —
+   otherwise the connect fails at runtime.
+3. A connect to a signal with **default arguments** must pick the full form the sender
+   actually emits; drop trailing defaulted params from the SLOT side only.
+4. The 31 overload-disambiguating casts
+   (`static_cast<void (QComboBox::*)(int)>(&QComboBox::currentIndexChanged)`) become
+   *simpler*: just write the chosen overload in the string.
+5. Free-function / non-QObject targets: new-style allows
+   `connect(sender, &S::sig, plainFunction)` — Qt4 does not. Wrap in a slot on the
+   nearest sensible QObject.
+6. Connects to `this`-less overloads (`connect(sender, &S::sig, someLambdaOrFunc)`)
+   need an explicit receiver in old syntax.
+7. `Qt::ConnectionType` arguments carry over unchanged as the 5th parameter.
+8. Convert the 21 `disconnect()` calls with the same rules.
+9. Convert the 11 connects in `tests/` too — the tests are your safety net; they must
+   build and run on Qt4.
+
+**Lambda connects — ~18 sites, no Qt4 equivalent, each needs a real (small) refactor**
+into a named private slot plus, where the lambda captured locals, member state. The
+audited list (single- and multi-line forms):
+`src/Cryptography/MessagePart.cpp` (crypto — will be Qt5-only anyway; still convert if
+trivial, else leave under the Qt5 guard), `src/Gui/CompleteMessageWidget.cpp`,
+`src/Gui/LineEdit.cpp`, `src/Gui/MailBoxTreeView.cpp` (2), `src/Gui/MessageHeadersWidget.cpp`,
+`src/Gui/MessageListWidget.cpp`, `src/Gui/MessageSourceWidget.cpp`, `src/Gui/MessageView.cpp`,
+`src/Gui/SimplePartWidget.cpp`, `src/Gui/Window.cpp` (3, incl. the `applicationState`
+one that Phase 2 guards out and the `singleShot` one). Grep for both
+`connect(.*\[` and multi-line `connect(...,$` + following-line `[` — the multi-line
+form hides from naive greps.
+
+**Verification of this phase specifically** (do all three):
+- Build the **Qt5** configuration after conversion — old syntax is still checked by
+  moc at runtime but at least the names/slots declarations get exercised; the build
+  must stay green.
+- Run the test suite on **both** Qt4 and Qt5 builds with `QT_FATAL_WARNINGS=1` in the
+  environment: any `QObject::connect: No such signal/slot` in a covered path becomes
+  a hard failure instead of a scrolled-past warning.
+- Mechanical audit: script a pass over your converted connects that extracts each
+  `SIGNAL(...)`/`SLOT(...)` string and greps the corresponding class declaration for
+  a matching member; investigate every miss by hand. (Inheritance means a plain grep
+  has false misses — chase the base classes rather than suppressing.)
+
+## Phase 4 — plugins
+
+v0.7 plugin classes use only `Q_PLUGIN_METADATA` (Qt5-only). v0.6 shows the exact dual
+pattern — replicate it for the three surviving plugins (AbookAddressbook,
+ClearTextPassword, QtKeyChainPassword): keep `Q_PLUGIN_METADATA` under a Qt5 guard in
+the header, and add the Qt4 export at the end of the .cpp as in
+`v0.6:src/Plugins/ClearTextPassword/ClearTextPassword.cpp:133`:
+`Q_EXPORT_PLUGIN2(trojita_plugin_ClearTextPasswordPlugin, trojita_plugin_ClearTextPasswordPlugin)`
+(guarded for Qt4; note v0.6 also has `Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN` lines for static consumers,
+e.g. `src/Plugins/AbookAddressbook/main.cpp:36`, and CMake generates
+`static_plugins.h` from the `TROJITA_STATIC_PLUGINS` property — restore that
+machinery from `v0.6:CMakeLists.txt` around line 838).
+
+The static path matters more than the shared one: on Qt4/macOS, runtime plugin
+loading via `QPluginLoader` is fragile (this is what broke the 0.6 port — silently,
+because every diagnostic in `PluginManager.cpp` is behind `#ifdef PLUGIN_DEBUG`), so
+the Portfile will build with `-DWITH_SHARED_PLUGINS=OFF`. **Test the Qt4 container
+build in both plugin modes**, and make sure the static Qt4 build actually registers
+the plugins (run the binary or a test that queries `PluginManager`;
+`QPluginLoader::staticInstances()` must contain them).
+
